 ask yourself how long  am I going to play this charade how long  can I take it or do i want to cooperate  with them find myself a little niche as  long as you're still studying you have  to acknowledge this craft in every essay  there had to be a commitment to  socialism and if you needed good marks  you just wrote that rubbish down a short  time later later attempted to escape  again he swam across a border River on  the edge of Berlin and made it to  freedom he was in the water for four  hours before he finally managed to get  through a barbed wire fence and onto the  shore on the western side on the eastern  side he would have been shot I had a  guardian angel that light I knew there  were alarms of the bridge in the  Twilight I saw a thin piece of wire  stretched from bridge post to bridge  post if I had touched it it would have  gone off and they would have immediately  fired shots because a faulty frozen  dozens of people died in this way along  the Berlin Wall  it's still not clear just how many the  regime ruthlessly punished anyone who  tried to flee nevertheless I wish to  continue to help more than thirty people  escape by smuggling them into West  Germany in the boot of his car he was  caught again and spent five years in  prison before he was bought out by the  West German government the 71 year old  now gives tours in his former prison he  thinks young people should learn that  East Germany was a totalitarian state  Mark DeRosa one mentioned you can see  the young people are aware and  interested that's why I do this out of a  sense of eviction I'll always be like  that before I am done bat stealing has  helped Hartmut Victor overcome the  brutality of the Stasi now he's able to  tell the story as one of those who paid  a price for wanting freedom  and I am joined now here in the studio  by axel Klaus Maia director of the  Berlin Wall memorial welcome to the  program and thank you for being with us  on this anniversary let's really start  from the very beginning remind us why  the wall was built to begin with well  there was the enormous pressure on these  German regimes in so many people escaped  the wall Germany as you know was divided  in 45 and especially during after 49 and  between the two German states were  founded and then between 1949 and the  actual date of the wall 1961 about a six  of the entire population had fled the  country and in that respect the  government or the regime was under  pressure and so apparently it saw as the  last exit at last a possibility to stop  people from escaping building a wall in  the center of Berlin since the inner  German border had been sealed off in  1952 already and it was really more than  just one wall was it wasn't it tell us a  little bit more about the fortification  and the area really surrounding it as  well it surrounded the entire West  Berlin so it was approximately a hundred  miles 155 kilometres and it was not I  mean the term the wall is actually  incorrect because it was a whole  sequence of obstacles a sequence of  different walls of perimeter defense but  also an electrical signal fence and a  watchtower a vehicle ditch and all of  this and of course as the actual and the  most dangerous part of it were the  border guards said after 1970 approached  1971-72 approximately 12,000 border  guards were on permanent duty around  West Berlin so only around West Berlin  of course the inner German border was  also protected and it was approximately  12,000 border guards at any given time  and yet people tried to escape some of  them successfully some of them you know  their attempts actually  ended up fatally they were killed do you  think that we will never ever know just  how many people tried to cross well we  have relatively reliable figures we know  that in Berlin alone we have 5000 people  that made it through the border  fortifications we are generally talking  about 50,000 people that crossed the  border but on the inner German border  and and in Berlin but of course the  people also found other ways to escape  so for example there we know of people  that escaped through third countries  like Romania or former Yugoslavia we  know of people who even crossed via  China so East Germans entered the West  German Embassy in Beijing for example  and then were flown out into the West so  it was a very long way from East Berlin  into the West via Beijing we have this  phenomena we have to say also is is that  not not everyone thinks that things were  all that bad in the former East in fact  there's there's a bit of nostalgia  amongst some sort of a longing for the  way that things used to be what do you  say to that well it is certainly a  phenomena that has been referred to many  times over the last thirty years and I  think there's nostalgia and it was  called Australia of mixed with eat with  the east in the German term certainly  something about when things change  abruptly when when there are very fast  changing movements and then all of a  sudden for example the entire political  system that surrounded you and this is  understandable but I do believe that  it's a bit over exaggerated because a  lot of people really very much value the  new Germany  and the majority of former East Germans  do value the new democratic Germany  thank you so much for joining us to  speak on this anniversary of the  beginning of building of the Berlin Wall  axl Klaus Meyer director of the Berlin  Wall memorial we appreciate you very  much
 

